1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,066
Wrongful convictions in shared failure banks.

2
00:00:03,817 --> 00:00:07,847
Why the memory of failure in automated science should keep its evidence.

3
00:00:10,233 --> 00:00:13,925
Autonomous research agents increasingly share a failure bank.

4
00:00:14,775 --> 00:00:19,845
Every failed experiment is written down once, so no agent wastes budget repeating it.

5
00:00:20,895 --> 00:00:22,239
That sounds sensible.

6
00:00:22,239 --> 00:00:28,319
But each failure verdict is a statistical claim, usually made from one small, noisy experiment.

7
00:00:30,417 --> 00:00:33,787
Take an idea that is genuinely good, but only slightly.

8
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:38,320
Measure it once, and the result scatters around its small true effect.

9
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:43,961
About a third of the time, the measurement lands below zero.

10
00:00:43,961 --> 00:00:46,535
The idea fails, and the bank marks it dead.

11
00:00:47,586 --> 00:00:53,632
Marginally good ideas are exactly the ones a single noisy test is most likely to convict.

12
00:00:55,717 --> 00:00:58,274
And the two possible errors are not symmetric.

13
00:00:58,924 --> 00:01:01,895
A bad idea that is wrongly kept gets built on.

14
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:05,835
Its descendants keep failing, and the mistake exposes itself.

15
00:01:06,785 --> 00:01:10,335
A good idea that is wrongly killed is never tried again.

16
00:01:10,335 --> 00:01:15,660
When new ideas grow from confirmed ones, none of its descendants are ever generated.

17
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:20,639
False positives expose themselves. False negatives never do.

18
00:01:22,733 --> 00:01:24,736
How often does this happen in practice?

19
00:01:25,386 --> 00:01:35,343
In a live training loop for a small character-level GPT, 17.5% of convictions were wrongful, close to the 19% our theory predicts.

20
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:43,722
Among near-miss ideas, ones that passed once and then failed verification, between a third and a half of the verdicts were wrong.

21
00:01:44,572 --> 00:01:51,066
And replaying 122,817 convictions on the public FCNet benchmark shows the same shape:

22
00:01:51,066 --> 00:01:55,191
the errors concentrate on small, genuine improvements.

23
00:01:57,283 --> 00:01:59,960
Do language-model agents look past the label?

24
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:05,573
We showed models the same one-run record, labeled either dead, or inconclusive.

25
00:02:06,323 --> 00:02:14,057
Labeled inconclusive, three Claude models put the chance the idea is genuinely better at 32.5%.

26
00:02:14,057 --> 00:02:15,603
Labeled dead: 8.5%.

27
00:02:16,553 --> 00:02:24,430
Across 12 models from 9 families, agents choosing their next experiment revisited none of 94 dead-marked ideas.

28
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:30,462
And every family picked the best available idea less often once it carried the label.

29
00:02:31,312 --> 00:02:34,855
The models can price the evidence. The label overrides it.

30
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:40,180
The fix is a change of schema. It needs no new model.

31
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:44,585
Instead of a bare dead flag, each record carries its evidence:

32
00:02:44,585 --> 00:02:47,916
the number of runs, the measured effect, and the noise.

33
00:02:48,666 --> 00:02:54,109
From that, the bank computes the probability that the idea is truly dead.

34
00:02:54,109 --> 00:02:56,346
One run, slightly worse: 0.79.

35
00:02:56,346 --> 00:03:02,012
That is below the threshold, so the idea stays open, and a retry is allowed.

36
00:03:02,962 --> 00:03:08,727
Five runs, averaging the same deficit: 0.96. Now the lock is earned.

37
00:03:09,676 --> 00:03:14,025
It is a standard confidence rule. Shared failure banks just don't implement it.

38
00:03:16,117 --> 00:03:21,222
Does it matter end to end? We ran a closed research loop with real training runs:

39
00:03:21,222 --> 00:03:23,807
8 independent loops for each memory rule.

40
00:03:24,557 --> 00:03:31,999
With no memory, with binary locks, or with a parole rule, each loop found about 46 truth-validated discoveries.

41
00:03:32,848 --> 00:03:39,288
With evidence-graded records: 60.5. That is 30–32% more;

42
00:03:39,288 --> 00:03:46,074
a difference of 13.9, with a 95% interval from 9.7 to 18.1.

43
00:03:47,025 --> 00:03:48,806
It does not hold everywhere.

44
00:03:48,806 --> 00:03:57,456
In a low-noise, short-horizon loop, binary locking edges ahead, and with language-model proposers, the ordering depends on the proposer.

45
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:03,046
Records that carry their evidence. Locks that require it.

46
00:04:03,996 --> 00:04:06,882
The code, logs, and cached runs are on GitHub.

